<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxerxes III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">assassinated <a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxerxes III was an Achaemenid king who reigned from 358 BCE until he was assassinated in 338 BCE. He suppressed revolts and reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 343 BCE, restoring royal authority. His murder by his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagoas">Bagoas</a> brought instability shortly before Alexander's invasion, contributing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>'s decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii.jpg" alt="Artaxerxes III" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AR VLD · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_relief_of_Artaxerxes_III_in_Persepolis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Artaxerxes III, whose personal name was Ochus, was the legitimate son of Artaxerxes II and his wife Stateira. Before he became king he was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> and commander of his father's army. The Roman writer Justin tells how his eldest brother Darius, the heir, was executed for plotting against their father, and how Ochus, with the support of some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, drove another brother, Ariaspes, to suicide and had the favoured illegitimate son Arsames killed. Ochus was then appointed crown prince. After taking the throne he murdered much of the royal family to secure his position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his accession he ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to disband their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> forces, to consolidate royal power. In 356 BCE he tried to dismiss Artabazus II from his satrapy, and Artabazus revolted, defeated royal satraps with Athenian and Theban help, and fled to Macedonia in 354 BCE. About 351 BCE Artaxerxes invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, supported by Greek mercenaries, defeated him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, Anatolia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> then declared independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrieus">Idrieus</a> of Caria reduced Cyprus, and Artaxerxes led a large army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennes">Tennes</a> tried to buy a pardon by betraying 100 citizens. The Persian king had them killed and then 500 more. Sidon was burnt and forty thousand people died.</p><p>After years of preparation Artaxerxes invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> again, led in person, with Greek mercenaries under Mentor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. He created three divisions of shock troops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a> surrendered, and Nectanebo abandoned the country. Egypt returned to Persian rule after about six decades. The king had city walls destroyed, looted temples and levied high taxes, and the native religion was persecuted. He also supported the city of Perinthus against the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late August or September 338 BCE the court eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagoas">Bagoas</a> had Artaxerxes poisoned through his physician. Bagoas had most of the king's sons murdered and put the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iv">Arses</a> on the throne as Artaxerxes IV. His death proved a problem for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and may have weakened the country, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> later conquered. Artaxerxes had begun a building programme at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, with a new palace and the Unfinished Gate, and his tomb was cut into the mountain behind the platform, next to his father's. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> coins issued while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> carry an inscription in an Egyptian script reading Artaxerxes Pharaoh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artaxerxes III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Greek Wars, a peripheral embarrassment from the Persian perspective, but with the loss of Egypt in 404 BCE, when the province revolted successfully at the death of Darius II and remained independent under native dynasties for some six decades until <b>Artaxerxes III</b> reconquered it in 343 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the Persian era, in 351 BCE, <b>Artaxerxes III</b> invaded Phoenicia, and in 333 BCE Alexander the Great occupied the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 351 BCE Sidon, under its king Tennes, led a revolt against the Persian king <b>Artaxerxes III</b>; when the Persian army approached, Tennes betrayed the city, and the Sidonians, Diodorus says, set fire to it rather than surrender.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kingship could be dangerous: Xerxes I was murdered in his bed, and <b>Artaxerxes III</b>, Greek writers say, was poisoned by the eunuch Bagoas in 338 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 343 BCE <b>Artaxerxes III</b> defeated Nectanebo II at Pelusium.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirtieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>After failed attempts in 351 BCE, the Persian king <b>Artaxerxes III</b> invaded Egypt in 343 BCE, defeating Nectanebo II of the Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt at Pelusium and capturing Memphis.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-338" aria-label="Babylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5690 981 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Artaxerxes III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 1251h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-338">What was here in 338 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
